{"id":2013,"date":"2020-12-11T01:56:29","date_gmt":"2020-12-11T09:56:29","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=2013"},"modified":"2024-09-01T14:01:12","modified_gmt":"2024-09-01T21:01:12","slug":"zumi-python-how-does-zumi-see","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/zumi-python-how-does-zumi-see\/","title":{"rendered":"2.1: How Does Zumi See?"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221522bdec4\" name=\"6a221522bdec4\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>How does Zumi see?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Self-driving cars need a lot more than just obstacle detection sensors. Human drivers have eyes and ears that help us see potential dangers up ahead that a proximity detector might not be able to detect. We can also tell the different between pedestrians, cyclists, and other cars. What else do self-driving cars need to navigate our world?[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221522bdfcd\" name=\"6a221522bdfcd\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Take a selfie<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]First up: use Zumi&#8217;s camera to take a picture and display it on the screen![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2014&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"Import-libraries\">Import libraries<\/h3>\n<p>Pay attention to the new libraries: the camera and vision libraries! These libraries contain code to take, modify, and display images.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from zumi.zumi import Zumi\r\nfrom zumi.util.screen import Screen\r\nimport cv2\r\nimport time\r\nfrom zumi.util.vision import Vision # New library!\r\nfrom zumi.util.camera import Camera # New library!\r\n\r\nzumi =\tZumi()\r\ncamera\t= Camera()\r\nscreen\t= Screen()\r\nvision\t= Vision()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221522be02c\" name=\"6a221522be02c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Cheese!<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Just like taking an actual picture, this code has a countdown so you can be prepared. Run the code, smile, and get ready to see yourself on the Zumi screen!<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">camera.start_camera()\t# Turn on the camera\r\n\r\nprint(\"3...\")\r\nscreen.draw_text_center(\"3...\")\r\ntime.sleep(1)\r\nprint(\"2...\")\r\nscreen.draw_text_center(\"2...\")\r\ntime.sleep(1)\r\nprint(\"1...\")\r\nscreen.draw_text_center(\"1...\")\r\ntime.sleep(1)\r\nscreen.draw_text_center(\"Cheese!\")\r\n\r\nimage = camera.capture()\t# Take a picture\r\ncamera.close()\t# Make sure to close the camera stream\r\nscreen.show_image(image)\t# Display image on OLED<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221522be0c9\" name=\"6a221522be0c9\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Displaying images in Jupyter<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Instead of showing your picture on the Zumi screen, display it in Jupyter Notebook. As a bonus, it will appear in color!<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">camera.start_camera()\r\n\r\nprint(\"3...\")\r\nscreen.draw_text_center(\"3...\")\r\ntime.sleep(1)\r\nprint(\"2...\")\r\nscreen.draw_text_center(\"2...\")\r\ntime.sleep(1)\r\nprint(\"1...\")\r\nscreen.draw_text_center(\"1...\")\r\ntime.sleep(1)\r\nscreen.draw_text_center(\"Cheese!\")\r\n\r\nframe = camera.capture()\r\ncamera.close()\r\n\r\ncamera.show_image(frame)<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221522be13c\" name=\"6a221522be13c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Camera functions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]There are three functions that you need to know for taking pictures with Zumi.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2017&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Before taking a picture, you will need to turn on the camera with start_camera(). You cannot take an image without the camera stream! The red light will indicate the camera is on. Next, use capture() to take a picture. Save the picture in a variable to display it later. For example,<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">frame = camera.capture()<\/pre>\n<p>Finally, don&#8217;t forget to turn off the camera! If you don&#8217;t run <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">close()<\/code>and you try to run <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">start_camera()<\/code>again, you will get an error. If this happens, save and close the notebook to force the camera to turn off.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221522be1a2\" name=\"6a221522be1a2\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Show the image<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Zumi doesn&#8217;t need to &#8220;see&#8221; the image like we do to process it because all she sees is numbers! To show what we mean, write code below that takes a picture and saves it in a variable, and then print the variable.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\"># Write code here!\r\ncamera.start_camera()\r\nimage = camera.capture()\r\ncamera.close()\r\n\u2022print(image)\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>However, humans need to see the image as pixels of color to understand it. As you saw in the code above, run camera.show_image() to show the image in Jupyter. Remember that this function takes in a parameter to know which image to show. In our example, we saved the image in the variable image. So to show the image we ran:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">camera.show image(image)<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221522be220\" name=\"6a221522be220\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Changing colorspaces<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]If you have ever played around with Photobooth or another photo editing program, you may have seen some interesting color filters that make your pictures change colors! Depending on the task, seeing the world differently actually helps computers process images faster. Notice that we shortened the variable name image to img since you will be using this variable a lot![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2018&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<strong>Grayscale<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Grayscale is what we would normally call &#8220;black and white&#8221;. However, this is not really accurate because the image is made up of gray pixels as well. Grayscale pictures are faster to process because there are no other colors. You will be using grayscale images later to scan QR codes!<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">camera.start_camera()\r\nimg = camera.capture()\r\ncamera.close()\r\ngray = vision.convert_to_gray(img) # Convert it to gray\r\ncamera.show_image(gray)\r\n<\/pre>\n<p><strong>HSV<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>HSV stands for <strong>hue<\/strong>, <strong>saturation<\/strong>, and <strong>value<\/strong>. Even though the image might look strange to you, this colorspace is useful for when Zumi needs to detect or track certain colors. It is more useful than the normal colored image that you are used to seeing because each pixel of information can tell the computer about the color&#8217;s intensity and whether or not there are shadows.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">camera.start_camera()\r\nimg = camera.capture()\r\ncamera.close()\r\nhsv = vision.convert_to_hsv(img) # Convert it to HSV, hue saturation and value\r\ncamera.show_image(hsv)\r\n<\/pre>\n<p><strong>Inverted<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>This one is just for fun! This filter inverts the tones of the color. For example, lighter areas become darker and darker areas become lighter.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">camera.start_camera()\r\nframe = camera.capture()\r\ncamera.close()\r\ninvert = cv2.bitwise_not(frame) # invert the colors\r\ncamera.show_image(invert)<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221522be271\" name=\"6a221522be271\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Resolution<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]You probably noticed that the pictures you displayed on Zumi&#8217;s screen were not very detailed. That is because the OLED screen is only 128 pixels wide and 64 pixels tall! You&#8217;ve heard us mention pixels before, but let&#8217;s look at an example. The first image is 770 pixels wide and 600 pixels tall. Each pixel is a little square of color. You can&#8217;t really see them until you zoom in.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2019&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Now look at the eyes more closely on the second image. In this image, you can see the individual pixels. There are 770 of them in one row and 600 in each column! If you had even more pixels, the picture would be considered a <strong>high-resolution <\/strong>image. In contrast, the resolution of the OLED is low.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2020&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221522be2e9\" name=\"6a221522be2e9\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Changing resolution<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Although you cannot change the resolution of the OLED, you can increase the resolution of the images that the camera takes. Run the next cell to take a picture (there is not a countdown this time so be ready!). What do you think the resolution is? Guess how many pixels wide and tall the image is below:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">camera.start_camera()\r\nimg = camera.capture()\r\ncamera.show_image(img)\r\ncamera.close()\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>Try changing these values below and watch your image stretch, shrink, and get bigger! Python may round up your pixel values to fit the ratios.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">width = 160 # &lt;-- CHANGE ME!\r\nheight = 128 # &lt;-- CHANGE ME!\r\n\r\ncamera = Camera(width,height) # Let the camera know what changes you are making!\r\ncamera.start_camera()\r\nimg = camera.capture()\r\ncamera.close()\r\n\r\ncamera.show_image(img)<\/pre>\n<p>There is a size limit! Here we will take a full-resolution image. You will notice that Zumi will take more time to process and display the image. Why do you think so?<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">width = 1296 # Largest resolution!\r\nheight = 976\r\n\r\ncamera = Camera(width,height)\r\ncamera.start_camera()\r\nimg = camera.capture()\r\ncamera.close()\r\n\r\ncamera.show_image(img)<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221522be34b\" name=\"6a221522be34b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 10<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Video<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Although a video looks seamless, a video is actually a series of pictures one aher the other. The images are shown so fast that you normally do not notice a difference. However, you may notice the difference here, especially if your images are very large. In order to display a video, take and display pictures inside of a for loop. Fill in the code below to show video. Since you will be using a loop, we are going to introduce two new sections of code that will help keep your program from crashing: <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">try<\/code> and <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">finally<\/code>.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">camera = Camera()\r\ncamera.start_camera()\r\n\r\ntry:\r\n    for x in range(30):\r\n        # TODO Take a picture\r\n        # TODO show the picture\r\n        camera.clear_output() # Clear the output for the next image to show\r\nfinally:\r\n    camera.close()<\/pre>\n<p>If anything goes wrong or you stop your code while in the try section, the program will automatically jump to the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">finally<\/code> statements. In this case, we put a <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">close()<\/code> for the camera so that you never have to worry about it staying on.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 11 How does Zumi see? [vc_column_text]Self-driving cars need a lot more than just obstacle detection sensors. Human drivers have eyes and ears that help us see potential dangers&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/zumi-python-how-does-zumi-see\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":2052,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"easy","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":810,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1789,1503,1482,514,853,996,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2236,2238,2237,2156,2122,2134,2239,2230],"class_list":["post-2013","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-camera","lesson-tag-does","lesson-tag-how","lesson-tag-intermediate","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-see","lesson-tag-zumi","module-intermediate","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/2013","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":5,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/2013\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":9149,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/2013\/revisions\/9149"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/2052"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=2013"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=2013"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=2013"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}